WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.220
500 endemic fish vanished from Lake Victoria after a stalking meant only to grow an export catch. This is unintended consequences, episode 161. This is the transfer that looked like ordinary fisheries practice. The long quiet before the perch took off, the loss of the grazers and the oxygen, the export economy that now blocks reversal, and the function nobody measured. In August 1954, officials from the Uganda Game and Fisheries Department moved Nile Perch from Lake Albert into Lake Victoria.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.240
scientists at the East African Fisheries Research Organization had

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.060
objected. The officials went ahead anyway. Among them was Alex M. Anderson.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.440
Anderson and his colleagues wanted a large predatory fish that people could

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.100
catch with simple gear and sell abroad. Lake Victoria already held hundreds of

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.320
small endemic haplacromine sicklids. Those fish fed local subsistence fishing.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.640
They looked too small and too fragmented for mechanized export. The officer's

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.880
The reason that a fast growing species already valued in other African waters would raise

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.600
the protein supply, create jobs, and bring in foreign exchange.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.060
That logic served both the colonial economy and the early post-independence years that followed.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.980
It also matched standard fisheries practice of the 1950s.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.600
When local stocks seemed underutilized, managers introduced a more marketable species instead

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.520
of managing the community already there.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.580
Air. Anderson's team treated the transfers as low-risk improvements to an underperforming

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:33.420
natural system. They saw the lake as untabbed biomass. One well-chosen addition, they believed,

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.260
could unlock that value. The era prized total yield, preserving every native interaction

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:43.820
was not the measure that counted. The first documented stockings went in near Ginger

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:51.000
on the Ugandan shore in August 1954. More fish were moved in 1955. Further official releases

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.800
This is followed in 1962 and 1963.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.880
Early catches stayed low for more than two decades.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.200
The project drew little public notice.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.580
Then Nile Perch numbers began rising sharply in the late 1970s.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.520
Kenyan waters recorded the first major increase around 1979.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.000
Uganda and Tanzania followed.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.100
Proponents pointed to rising total fish landings.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.280
To them, the landings proved the introduction had succeeded.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.940
A few biologists had warned that the predator might reduce the diversity of the smaller

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.760
sicklids. Those concerns were set aside. The economic payoff looked large, and it looked immediate.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:32.680
The lag between stalking and explosion did something else. By the time the purge population took off,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.240
the original decision-makers had moved on. New managers, watching export statistics, saw an

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:46.574
unqualified success. Between 1979 and 1987, Nile Purge densities exploded. The predator rapidly

00:02:46.574 --> 00:02:51.374
replace the native haplacromine assemblage. That assemblage had made up the bulk of the

00:02:51.374 --> 00:02:57.214
fish biomass. Research published in Pios I in 2013 shows the timing of the replacement.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:03:02.934
It matches the predator's logistic growth curve, not any single external trigger. The

00:03:02.934 --> 00:03:07.514
sicklids that ate phytoplankton and detritus had been the lake's grazers. Without that

00:03:07.514 --> 00:03:12.694
grazing pressure, blue-green algae proliferated. Studies in the Canadian Journal of Fisheries

00:03:12.694 --> 00:03:17.834
and aquatic sciences, and in conservation biology, link the loss of those grazers directly

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:21.254
to increased algal blooms and to the hypoxia that followed.

00:03:21.914 --> 00:03:25.034
Large portions of the lake now experience seasonal anoxia.

00:03:25.694 --> 00:03:28.714
The Moonsa Gulf and the deeper basins are especially affected.

00:03:29.294 --> 00:03:32.054
The dead water kills fish and alters nutrient cycling.

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:37.834
The new fishery concentrated profits among owners of larger boats and among Nile perch processing

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:38.234
plants.

00:03:38.234 --> 00:03:43.654
Smaller-scale fishers who had relied on diverse sickled catches lost their primary resource.

00:03:44.114 --> 00:03:48.794
An African Journal of Aquatic Science review traces endemic haplochromines to extinction

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:52.954
or near extinction, and with them the lake's original food web structure.

00:03:53.474 --> 00:03:57.754
These were not immediate side effects, they were second-order results of removing the dominant

00:03:57.754 --> 00:04:00.334
grazers that had kept the water clear for millennia.

00:04:00.814 --> 00:04:04.994
The chain runs from the predator's numerical increase to the disappearance of algae-eating

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:05.414
fish.

00:04:05.414 --> 00:04:09.014
then to unchecked plant growth, then to oxygen depletion.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:13.874
That depletion then blocks recovery of the very species predation had already reduced.

00:04:14.414 --> 00:04:19.074
Industrial filating plants and refrigerated transport rewarded operators who could handle

00:04:19.074 --> 00:04:20.514
large, uniform catches.

00:04:21.114 --> 00:04:25.654
The economic landscape tilted further away from the mixed species canoe fishery that had

00:04:25.654 --> 00:04:26.514
existed before.

00:04:27.631 --> 00:04:32.231
Lake Victoria's Nile Perch fishery now supports several million livelihoods across Uganda,

00:04:32.891 --> 00:04:34.031
Kenya, and Tanzania.

00:04:34.031 --> 00:04:36.851
The ecosystem remains fundamentally altered.

00:04:37.391 --> 00:04:41.051
No practical method exists to remove the predator at the scale of the lake.

00:04:41.671 --> 00:04:46.551
Repeated attempts to restock or protect remaining sicklids have produced only local temporary

00:04:46.551 --> 00:04:46.951
gains.

00:04:47.671 --> 00:04:51.231
Industrial plants export frozen filets to Europe and the Middle East.

00:04:51.851 --> 00:04:54.451
Local protein availability has become more variable.

00:04:55.071 --> 00:04:59.531
Recent management talk centers on effort controls and size limits, not on reversal.

00:05:00.211 --> 00:05:02.451
The original introduction cannot be undone.

00:05:02.451 --> 00:05:06.811
the same export-oriented system that rewarded larger vessels still shapes

00:05:06.811 --> 00:05:11.631
investment. Attempts to impose minimum mesh sizes or closed seasons have met

00:05:11.631 --> 00:05:16.111
resistance from processors who need steady volume. The infrastructure built around

00:05:16.111 --> 00:05:20.727
the introduced species now limits which corrections are even possible. When

00:05:20.727 --> 00:05:25.187
Anderson's team added one high-value predator, the reward was a narrow measure,

00:05:25.727 --> 00:05:30.327
total export tonnage. The lake's internal grazing function went unmeasured and

00:05:30.327 --> 00:05:35.007
protected. Later managers who watched only Nile Perch biomass repeated that

00:05:35.007 --> 00:05:39.807
accounting. Oxygen crashes were allowed to persist. The same pattern shows up

00:05:39.807 --> 00:05:44.067
wherever a new species is stalked or a large intervention is judged by the

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:48.687
single output someone can sell. Yield rises on the ledger. The work the

00:05:48.687 --> 00:05:52.647
resident species were already doing never appears on it. If you are weighing

00:05:52.647 --> 00:05:56.547
an introduction or any intervention that replaces an existing community with

00:05:56.547 --> 00:06:02.227
something easier to harvest, ask what measurable function those species perform, ask how that

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:07.127
function would be tracked before the newcomer succeeds and the change can no longer be walked back.

00:06:07.687 --> 00:06:12.667
Environmental intelligence follows the same kind of gap when a regulation or a restoration

00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:17.707
counts the commodity and misses the process holding the water, the soil, or the food web together.

00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:23.107
What would you insist on measuring first? We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking

00:06:23.107 --> 00:06:24.107
through this one with me.

